= Choosing Our Opponents

In our campaigns and in the media, who should we treat as our main opponent?

[? if enemies != 0 : So far, our main opponent has been the [+ enemies +]. Is a change of direction necessary? ?]

- @anti_dem: The populists of the far right and the far left: the AfD and the BSW.
- @far_right: The far right: the AfD.
- @linke: The Linke.
- @bourgeois: The centre-right: the CDU/CSU and the FDP.
- @easy_discard

@anti_dem
on-arrival: enemies = "populist parties"; center_strength += 5; reformist_strength += 5; left_dissent += 5; workers_afd -= 2; workers_bsw -= 2; rural_bsw -= 2; bsw_relation -= 10

Attacking the populists of both sides is popular with most of the party. The left would rather fight the centre-right, and we lose any chance of working with the BSW.

@far_right
on-arrival: enemies = "AfD"; left_strength += 3; left_dissent -= 5; afd_urgency += 1; workers_afd -= 3; new_middle_afd -= 1; afd_relation -= 5

[? if afd_r < 10 : The AfD is weaker than it was, and some in our party ask why we keep giving it attention. ?][? if afd_r >= 10 : The whole party can agree on this. The right of the party says that we should be just as clear about the Linke, and some in the Union see it as an attack on them for their talks with the AfD. ?]

@linke
on-arrival: enemies = "Linke"; reformist_strength += 5; left_dissent += 8; reformist_dissent -= 5; linke_relation -= 15; fdp_relation += 5; cdu_relation += 5; workers_linke -= 3; new_middle_linke -= 2

Attacking the Linke wins applause from the Seeheimer and from the centre-right. But if we ever want a left majority, this may not be the best choice.

@bourgeois
on-arrival: enemies = "centre-right"; left_strength += 8; left_dissent -= 5; fdp_relation -= 5; cdu_relation -= 5; linke_relation += 5; reformist_dissent += 8; workers_cdu -= 2; coalition_dissent += 1 if cdu_in_government or fdp_in_government

Attacking the Union and the FDP sharpens our profile on the left, but it may make the coalitions we need harder to build.
